[Music]  so let's talk a little bit about sight  reading it's an extensive topic but i  just want to focus in on a few  strategies that help me out when i'm  sight reading for myself or with others  the first thing is to gather as much  information from the score before you  start playing this includes the time  signature  the key signature  tempo and also just the general gist of  the notes  in terms of the flow certain  characteristics that that pop out to you  visually the second thing is to really  prioritize rhythm and pulse  these will serve as your anchors when  you're sight reading and when you're  faced with things that you're unfamiliar  with or you're tempted to hesitate this  will keep you grounded the third thing  is to read in chunks both vertically and  horizontally  what i mean by this is to recognize sets  of notes within a chord or chords and  sets of notes within a phrase so here  instead of reading a e  a e  e c sharp looking at those as fifth  fifth sixth is much more efficient also  with phrasing this way it's possible to  look at a set of notes as one entity  [Music]  so let's take for example a sentence in  the english language  yesterday my dog ate a giant cupcake so  the strategy here is to recognize that  whole thing as a sentence and take it in  as one thing so the fourth thing is to  notice patterns and sequences this will  go a long way because to some degree you  know what to expect and almost predict  in terms of what's coming next  and also it helps you reset per module  so say that it's a pattern like here  if you screw up somewhere it's easy to  just reset in the next little module  the fifth thing is to be aware of  harmonic centers and also just the  language of the composer in general this  type of information really helps  organize the score for you and also  helps you make sense of different  accidentals and different  types of groups or whatever you see on  the page  [Music]  so this can be as simple as noticing  that certain notes have sharps or flats  in them in a consistent manner so the  sixth point is to use all the five  things that i mentioned previously  to fake in a way that  is less wrong  than the worst  type of wrong i don't know if that makes  sense but with sight reading i believe  that there is a healthy amount of  faking that is acceptable so let's go  back to that sentence example if you  glanced at that sentence and just took  three words from it and those were  yesterday eight giant  that is so much farther from the actual  meaning of the sentence than if you were  to extract dog ate cupcake  again three these three words versus  these three words  same thing with music  so going back to talking about pulse and  rhythm it's far worse to play something  really softly in the wrong rhythm than  it is to play it in the right rhythm  if you come across a chord or  something in the page that you're  absolutely not sure of just leave it out  so that's it and i'm gonna go practice  this so that i'm not sight reading it  during rehearsal  [Music]

